/* =============================================================================
   PUNJAB — data for state-tool-engine.js. See assets/js/data/kerala.js for the
   schema reference, and assets/js/data/haryana.js / rajasthan.js for the prior
   examples of this file's research/honesty discipline (primary-source PDF
   extraction, rule-by-rule citation, honest hedging over invented figures,
   and independently resolving each state's current NPS/OPS status rather
   than assuming).

   THE PROVENANCE QUESTION THIS FILE WAS SPECIFICALLY ASKED TO RESOLVE —
   UNLIKE HARYANA, PUNJAB NEVER REWROTE PCSR; IT STILL RUNS ON THE ORIGINAL
   "PUNJAB CIVIL SERVICES RULES" UNDER THAT SAME NAME, AS AMENDED: this
   session downloaded all three volumes directly from finance.punjab.gov.in's
   own Acts & Rules page (finance.punjab.gov.in/ActsnRules/Index) — "THE
   PUNJAB CIVIL SERVICES RULES, Volume I" (Parts I & II — Pay, Allowances,
   Leave, Joining Time; "Revised Edition", amended upto 31st January, 2016),
   "Volume II" (Pensions, Provident Funds and General/Group Insurance
   Scheme; "Fourth Edition", corrected upto 31st January, 2016), and
   "Volume III" (Travelling Allowance Rules; "Fifth Edition", amended upto
   31st January, 2016) — all four PDFs extracted to clean, directly-readable
   text via pdftotext with NO OCR needed (a genuine contrast with the
   legacy-Hindi-font garbling chhattisgarh.js/rajasthan.js had to fight
   through OCR — these PCSR volumes are typeset in plain English with no
   non-Unicode font problem at all). Every rule number, sub-clause and
   day-count below is a direct read of that text. Punjab is, in other words,
   the mirror image of Haryana: Haryana renamed/renumbered its inherited
   PCSR into nine new "Haryana Civil Services Rules, 2016" instruments with
   explicit repeal clauses; Punjab kept "Punjab Civil Services Rules" as the
   live, current, actively-administered name and citation for its own
   leave/pension/GPF/insurance code — the Department of Finance's own website
   hosts these volumes today, not a superseded historical text. One caveat
   honestly flagged throughout below: the compiled editions in hand are
   "amended/corrected upto 31st January, 2016" — genuinely current in the
   sense of being what the Department itself still hosts and cites, but this
   session could not confirm whether every individual rule has been further
   amended since 2016 (Punjab's own pay-revision timeline is unusually late
   and staggered — see the NPS/OPS section below — which makes several
   rupee-figure provisions, in particular the pension/gratuity ceilings in
   Volume II, read as pre-revision figures rather than current ones; flagged
   individually where it matters).

   NPS / OPS STATUS — THE OTHER FINDING THIS SESSION WAS SPECIFICALLY ASKED
   TO RESOLVE, AND A GENUINELY NARROWER, MORE TECHNICAL ANSWER THAN
   RAJASTHAN'S OR CHHATTISGARH'S BLANKET NPS-TO-OPS SWAPS FOR ALL POST-2004
   RECRUITS — DO NOT ASSUME A BLANKET RESTORATION: PCSR Volume I, Part I,
   Rule 1.2(1)'s own proviso confirms the baseline: "the rules in Part I —
   Pensions and the Punjab General Provident Fund Rules contained in
   Volume-II... shall not apply to the Government employees who are
   appointed to [State Service] posts... on or after the 1st January, 2004.
   They shall be covered by [a] new 'Defined Contributory Pension Scheme'"
   (i.e. NPS) — matching Rule 13.1 of the GPF chapter itself, which states in
   equally plain terms that the GPF Rules "do not apply to the Government
   employees who were appointed on or after the first day of January, 2004."
   Punjab's AAP Government did announce an Old Pension Scheme restoration —
   widely reported in November 2022 (Cabinet approval/notification dated
   18.11.2022, reported at the time as covering "over 1.75 lakh" employees
   "presently... covered under NPS") — but the actual, later Finance
   Department paperwork this session was able to read directly narrows that
   considerably. This session located and OCR'd (Tesseract, -l pan+eng — the
   Gurmukhi portions of these circulars were NOT relied on, only the English
   text, which OCR'd cleanly) two independent copies of the same underlying
   Finance Department circular being forwarded down different administrative
   chains (one via a Punjab school-education directorate's own memo dated
   6.6.2025, re-forwarding Finance's letter; a second, fuller copy on
   Finance's own letterhead) — Government of Punjab, Department of Finance
   (Finance Pension Policy and Coordination Branch), Letter No.
   FD-FPPCOCMPS/41/2020-2FPPC/1112977/2025, dated 28.05.2025, on the subject
   "Benefit of Old Pension Scheme for those Punjab Government employees who
   are appointed against the posts/vacancies advertised for recruitment,
   before 01.01.2004 and Government employees who are appointed on
   compassionate grounds on or after 01.01.2004, but the request for
   appointment for the post was received before 01.01.2004" — forwarding an
   E-Gazette Notification No. G.S.R.34/Const./Arts.309 and 187/Amd.(11)/2025
   dated 22.05.2025 (i.e. an amendment to the Punjab Civil Services Pension
   Rules made under the Governor's Article 309 rule-making power). Read
   plainly, this is NOT "every employee currently on NPS moves to OPS" — it
   is specifically employees whose RECRUITMENT PROCESS (the vacancy
   advertisement, or a compassionate-appointment request) predates
   1.1.2004, even though their actual joining, and hence their NPS
   enrolment, came later. The same circular separately states that for
   Boards/Corporations/Autonomous Bodies, whether to extend the same OPS
   benefit to their own NPS-enrolled staff is left to each organisation
   "subject to the provision made in the[ir] bylaws, service rules and their
   own respective financial health," with the State Government explicitly
   NOT bearing any resulting additional financial burden for those bodies.
   IMPORTANT LIMITATION: this session read only these secondary-hosted
   reproductions of the Finance Department's own circular and memo (via
   employeesforum.online / ssapunjab.org mirrors, not fetched directly from
   punjab.gov.in/egazette or finance.punjab.gov.in themselves — this
   session's WebSearch budget was exhausted before broader corroboration
   could be attempted, and the E-Gazette notification G.S.R.34/Const./
   Arts.309 and 187/Amd.(11)/2025 itself was not independently located and
   read) — the circular's own letterhead, reference number, dates and
   Gazette-notification citation are internally consistent across both
   independently-forwarded copies OCR'd this session, which is the basis for
   treating it as reliable, but it has not been cross-verified against the
   primary Gazette text itself. Net effect for this file's 'service'
   category: an employee should not assume "I am on NPS, therefore OPS now
   applies to me" — eligibility turns on the DATE THE RECRUITMENT PROCESS
   FOR THEIR OWN POST BEGAN, not simply their date of joining or current
   scheme; the OPS/NPS Status Confirmation type below is written to surface
   this distinction rather than assert a blanket answer.

   CASUAL LEAVE, QUARANTINE LEAVE AND SPECIAL CASUAL LEAVE ARE PART OF PCSR
   ITSELF (APPENDIX 17), NOT A SEPARATE GAD CIRCULAR — ANOTHER GENUINE
   STRUCTURAL DIFFERENCE FROM RAJASTHAN/GUJARAT/CHHATTISGARH/MAHARASHTRA:
   Rule 8.61, Chapter VIII (Leave) of PCSR Volume I, Part I, points expressly
   to "Appendix 17 in Part II of this Volume" for "Rules for the grant of
   Casual and Quarantine Leave" — and that Appendix 17 (read in full, Volume
   I Part II) also contains Special Casual Leave, in five numbered
   categories: Family Welfare Schemes (sterilisation etc.), Sports Events,
   Office-Bearers of recognised Service Associations/trade unions, Cultural
   Events/Programmes, and Ex-servicemen appearing before a Medical Re-survey
   Board or requiring Artificial Limb Centre treatment, plus a sixth,
   free-standing provision for victims of terrorist violence. This is a
   stronger evidentiary footing for Punjab's Casual/Quarantine/Special
   Casual Leave figures than the several states in this app where that
   circular could not even be located.

   GENUINELY DISTINCTIVE FIGURES, DIRECT FROM THE RULE TEXT — flagged because
   they differ materially from this app's other state files: Earned Leave
   accumulation is capped at 450 days (Rule 8.116(ii)) — higher than
   Rajasthan's 300 and Chhattisgarh's typical 300, though lower than
   Haryana's uncapped-with-grant-ceiling model. Child Care Leave is capped at
   ONLY 365 days (one year) total over the entire service (Rule 8.128-B(1))
   — a full year LESS than Haryana's and Rajasthan's 730-day (two-year) cap
   — and, on the text read this session, remains restricted to WOMEN
   employees only, with no single-male-employee extension of the kind
   Rajasthan's Rule 103-C or Madhya Pradesh's 2025 Rules provide. Adoption
   Leave (Rule 8.128-A) is narrower than most peer states in a different way:
   it is available only to a woman employee who "does not have her own
   children" (not merely "fewer than 2," as in Haryana/Rajasthan) adopting a
   NEWLY BORN child specifically, for up to 3 months from adoption or until
   the child turns 3 months old, whichever is earlier — materially shorter
   and narrower than Haryana's/Rajasthan's up-to-6-months, under-1-year-old
   provisions. Maternity Leave itself (Rule 8.127(a)) is comparatively
   generous by contrast — full pay, up to 180 days, denied only to a woman
   with THREE OR MORE living children (most peer states in this app cut off
   at two) — while its own miscarriage/abortion extension (Note 2 below Rule
   8.127(a)) reverts to the more usual "fewer than two living children" cut-
   off, a genuine internal asymmetry in the rule text worth flagging rather
   than smoothing over. Paternity Leave (Rule 8.128(1)) is stated in the
   primary text in an unusual, slightly opaque way — "may be granted
   Paternity Leave upto a maximum of 15 days (after commuting half pay leave
   of 30 days)" — this session reproduces that phrasing as written rather
   than guessing at its intended mechanics; it is flagged as ambiguous in
   the type's own hint rather than silently resolved one way or the other.

   INSURANCE — the "Punjab Government Employees Group Insurance Scheme,
   1982" is confirmed by name and administered under PCSR Volume II, Chapter
   XV (Rules 15.1-15.4), itself replacing the "Punjab Civil Services (Group
   Insurance) Rules, 1982" (Notification No. 7(10)-OSD(F)-82/6494, dated
   15th August 1982) which Rule 15.4 repeals and savings-clauses forward.
   HOWEVER: the actual scheme document — Appendix VII, where Rule 15.1(b)
   itself says the Scheme "is set out" — is marked "(Not Printed)" in this
   very compilation's own Appendix index (page-list entry for Appendix VII,
   Volume II). That means this session could confirm the scheme's name,
   legal basis, membership rules (compulsory for post-1987 entrants, opt-out
   available for pre-1987 entrants, and a separate election mechanism for
   All-India Service officers between this scheme and the Central Government
   Employees Group Insurance Scheme, 1980) — but NOT any current
   subscription/premium rate, sum-assured formula, or Savings Fund
   accumulation table, because the source document that would contain them
   is not printed in the edition this session read. No premium or cover
   rupee figure is asserted below.

   ADVANCES — DELIBERATELY OMITTED AS A WHOLE CATEGORY, WITH REASONS: unlike
   Rajasthan's file (which found at least a notification number for House
   Building Advance even without a rupee figure), this session could not
   find ANY Punjab-specific evidence for House Building Advance, Motor
   Vehicle Advance, or Festival Advance — no PCSR rule, no Finance Department
   order number, nothing via web search (which repeatedly surfaced only
   Central Government or other-state schemes, or nothing at all). The
   "Punjab Financial Rules" (PFR), Parts I and II — also hosted on
   finance.punjab.gov.in's Acts & Rules page, and the most likely home for
   these provisions by analogy to Rajasthan's Treasury Rules cross-references
   — turned out to be very large (335 pages / 89 MB for Part I alone; 55 MB
   for Part II), apparently scanned-image PDFs with NO text layer at all
   (pdftotext produced zero output; this is a different failure mode from
   the legacy-Hindi-font garbling documented in rajasthan.js/chhattisgarh.js
   — there was no text to garble). A full-document OCR pass over either
   volume was judged too slow to be worth the time budget for this session
   (per this task's own explicit instruction not to get stuck on a stubborn
   scanned PDF); a 10-page table-of-contents OCR sample did not surface an
   advances chapter. Rather than invent a scheme name, ceiling, or interest
   rate for Punjab specifically, this whole category is left out — a more
   conservative choice than Rajasthan's for the same category, but the
   honest one given the evidence actually in hand this session.

   ESTATE / FAMILY PENSION — Rules 6.17 (admissibility/normal rate), 6.17-A
   (enhanced rate on death in service) and 6.16-AA (Death-cum-Retirement
   Gratuity) of PCSR Volume II were read in full: normal-rate family pension
   on death after retirement is 40% of pay (minimum Rs. 3,500/month) where
   the deceased's retirement pay did not exceed Rs. 10,000, or 30% of pay
   (minimum Rs. 4,000/month) above that threshold, "effective from the 1st
   day of January, 2006" (Rule 6.17(1)); the enhanced rate on death IN
   SERVICE is 60% of pay (pay not exceeding Rs. 10,000) or 50% of pay,
   minimum Rs. 6,000/month (pay above Rs. 10,000), payable for 15 years or
   until the deceased would have turned 65, whichever is less (Rule 6.17-A).
   Death-cum-Retirement Gratuity is capped at Rs. 10 lakh, with emoluments
   for this purpose themselves capped at Rs. 77,000 (Rs. 85,000 where
   Non-Practising Allowance is drawn) (Rule 6.16-AA(3), (5)). IMPORTANT: all
   of these are 2006-dated (or earlier) figures preserved in a compilation
   only "corrected upto 31st January, 2016" — given Punjab's own unusually
   late and staggered pay-revision timeline (a 2020 Finance Department
   notification found this session shows 7th-CPC-equivalent scales being
   phased in for new recruits from 17.7.2020, well over a decade after most
   states), these rupee figures and thresholds are very likely superseded by
   later revision but NO current figure is asserted — confirm with the
   Directorate of Pensions, Government of Punjab, before relying on any
   amount here. No distinct, dedicated indemnity-bond form number
   (analogous to Kerala's K.F.C. Form No. 9) for a deceased employee's
   general dues was found — the only indemnity-bond reference located is
   "Form A," used specifically where a de facto (non-court-appointed)
   guardian claims family pension on behalf of a minor child (Rule 6.17,
   Note 4) — narrower than a general dues-claim bond; flagged in that type's
   hint rather than assumed to extend further.

   DELIBERATELY OMITTED, WITH REASONS — Study Leave (Rule 8.126) is omitted
   for the same reason every other state file in this app omits it: no prior
   state file models it as an applicant-facing type. Leave to Probationers
   and Apprentices (Rules 8.130-8.139, "Part B" of Chapter VIII) is not
   modelled as its own type — Rule 8.133 applies the same Rules 8.116-8.119
   (Earned/Half Pay/Commuted Leave) to probationers/apprentices, so the
   ordinary leave types above already cover it. "Leave beyond the date of
   retirement" is not separately modelled; its accompanying "Encashment of
   earned leave" provision (Rule 8.21) IS modelled, as Earned Leave
   Encashment on Retirement, below. The Punjab Financial Rules (PFR)
   themselves, and hence any Permanent Advance/Imprest provisions distinct
   from the omitted employee-loan advances discussed above, are left out for
   the same access-limitation reasons.
   ============================================================================= */
